### Case 2: $c\ne 0$

From ($\star$) at $x=0$: $f(-c)\,f(c)=0$.

From (1) at $x=c$: $c(f(c)-c)=f(0)\,f(f(c))=c\,f(f(c))$, so $f(f(c))=f(c)-c$.  
From (2) at $y=c$: $c(f(c)-c)=f(-f(c))\,f(0)=c\,f(-f(c))$, so $f(-f(c))=f(c)-c$.

Let $d:=f(c)$. Then $f(d)=f(-d)=d-c$.

**If $f(c)=0$:** then $d=0$, $f(f(c))=f(0)=c$ but $f(c)-c=-c$, so $c=-c\Rightarrow c=0$. Contradiction.

**So $f(c)\ne0$ and $f(-c)=0$.** Setting $y=-c$ in the original (using $f(-c)=0$):
$$(x+c)\,f(x)=f(x)\,f(f(x)+c).$$
For $x$ with $f(x)\ne0$: $\;f(f(x)+c)=x+c$. $\quad$(4)

Apply (4) at $x=c$: $f(d+c)=2c$.  
Apply (4) at $x=d$ (if $f(d)\ne0$, i.e. $d\ne c$): $f(f(d)+c)=d+c$, i.e. $f((d-c)+c)=f(d)=d+c$. But $f(d)=d-c$, so $d-c=d+c\Rightarrow c=0$. Contradiction.  
If $d=c$: then $f(c)=c=d$ and $f(d)=d-c=0$, but $f(d)=f(c)=c\ne0$; contradiction.

All subcases yield $c=0$, contradicting $c\ne0$. **Case 2 is impossible.**

---

### Conclusion

The only functions satisfying the equation are $\boxed{f(x)=0\text{ for all }x,\quad\text{and}\quad f(x)=x\text{ for all }x.}$

